{
  "_meta": {
    "description": "Amharic experiencer (psych) verbs. They do not inflect like regular verbs: the active normally carries an object clitic (ደነቀው 'it amazed him'), the ተ- passive carries the same experiencer meaning without one (ተደነቀ 'he was amazed'), and the imperative strongly prefers the ተ- form (ተቸገር, rarely ቸግረው). So an ACTIVE form with an object clitic is a perfective, not an imperative.",
    "key": "consonantal skeleton of the citation root: phonology.consonantal_skeleton(root). Vowel order is ignored, so ቸገር / ቸገረ / ቸግር all key to ቸገረ. The KEY is the skeleton; the 'root' field always holds the real citation spelling.",
    "invariant": "consonantal_skeleton(entry['root']) == the entry's key, for every entry.",
    "bound": "true when the verb has no basic (unprefixed) form and only occurs with a voice prefix.",
    "causatives": "direct_causative is the አ- derivation, indirect_causative the አስ- derivation. Each is a string (the form), false (confirmed absent), or null (unknown). 'causative_source' says where it came from: 'corpus' = attested as a Simple Perfect cell in data/10_column_verbs.tsv; 'user' = supplied 2026-10-08; 'derived' = አስ- prefixed by rule and NOT verified - treat as a hypothesis to check, not a fact; null = unknown. Causatives were matched by EXACT string only: skeleton matching produces false positives, e.g. አረደ (469) is 'slaughter' and አቋጠ (1419) is 'cut off', not causatives of ረዳ and ቆጣ.",
    "source": "user-supplied lexical list (2026-10-08); corpus rows from data/10_column_verbs.tsv",
    "usage": "morphology.AmharicMorphologyEngine.is_experiencer_root()",
